    {
        "content": "<p><code>mspec (prog_spec1 a).and _ (prog_spec2 b)</code> works here. Perhaps there's nicer tactic support for this - I'm not sure - but the logic certainly allows it</p>",
        "id": 581133627,
        "sender_full_name": "Bhavik Mehta",
        "timestamp": 1774276819
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks, I am trying to understand the philosophy of this hoare triple reasoning, and still feel like not having the full picture.</p>",
        "id": 581146610,
        "sender_full_name": "Mirek Olšák",
        "timestamp": 1774279613
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Btw, do you know if this <code>m</code> in all the <code>m*</code> tactics stands for <code>monadic</code>, or <code>modal</code> (or both)?</p>",
        "id": 581149914,
        "sender_full_name": "Mirek Olšák",
        "timestamp": 1774280149
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I've always thought of it as <code>monadic</code></p>",
        "id": 581155259,
        "sender_full_name": "Bhavik Mehta",
        "timestamp": 1774281161
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>But the <code>SPred</code> concept is general enough to reason about arbitrary modal logic, right?</p>",
        "id": 581156562,
        "sender_full_name": "Mirek Olšák",
        "timestamp": 1774281456
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>A bit late to all of this, but yes, m is for \"monadic\". This makes sense for <code>mvcgen</code> and <code>mspec</code>. But for the SPred proof mode, I initially wanted s for \"stateful\", because that's really what SPred is about. But <code>sexists</code> and <code>shave</code> were a bit too... funnily overloaded.</p>\n<p>Combining multiple specs is indeed possible. If you want to use mvcgen (which I recommend), then you can have-bind the combined specs and use the combination like <code>mvcgen [combined]</code>.</p>",
        "id": 583366696,
        "sender_full_name": "Sebastian Graf",
        "timestamp": 1775166848
    }
]